WEBVTT

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:17.790
<v Ira Glass>Hey, everybody, Ira Glass here. So we've been working on today's episode of the radio show for a while, and the theme of the episode is "Tarred and Feathered." And initially, we just assumed we'd be using the phrase "tarred and feathered" as a metaphor for when somebody is shamed publicly.
<v Ira Glass>大家好，我是艾拉·格拉斯。我们一直在筹备今天这期电台节目，主题是“柏油与羽毛”（Tarred and Feathered，意指被当众羞辱）。起初，我们只是想把这个短语作为一种隐喻，形容某人当众出丑。

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:33.100
<v Ira Glass>But then we read a news report about a guy named Jock Nelson, who actually was tarred and feathered. Somebody poured tar on him and then stuck feathers to the tar, and not in the year 1850 or something. This was in 2007, seven years ago, in Belfast in Northern Ireland.
<v Ira Glass>但随后我们读到了一篇关于一个叫乔克·尼尔森的人的新闻报道，他真的遭遇了柏油与羽毛的惩罚。有人把柏油浇在他身上，然后粘上羽毛。这可不是发生在1850年什么的，这是在2007年，七年前，发生在北爱尔兰的贝尔法斯特。

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:35.620
<v Jon Ronson>Where did it happen? Was it just around that corner?
<v Jon Ronson>事情发生在什么地方？就在那个街角吗？

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:38.500
<v Jackie Mcdonald>Just-- well-- 100 yards from here.
<v Jackie Mcdonald>就在——嗯——离这儿100码的地方。

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:44.710
<v Jon Ronson>OK, so I'm looking over to where you're pointing. And there's a day nursery, the Scribbles Day Nursery.
<v Jon Ronson>好，我正看向你指的方向。那边有个日托中心，涂鸦日托中心（Scribbles Day Nursery）。

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:57.990
<v Ira Glass>Jon Ronson, who appears on our show from time to time, happened to be going to Belfast this week and happens to be writing a book about public shaming. So we asked him to go check out where this happened, which, yes, was right in front of a daycare center.
<v Ira Glass>乔恩·龙森，我们节目的常客，这周正好要去贝尔法斯特，而且恰好在写一本关于公众羞辱的书。所以我们请他去看看事发现场，没错，就在一家托儿所门前。

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:01.250
<v Jon Ronson>He was tied to a post. Was it that post right there that he was tied to, that lamp post?
<v Jon Ronson>他被绑在柱子上。是绑在那个柱子上吗，那根灯柱？

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:16.790
<v Jackie Mcdonald>Yup. The woman actually wanted to beat him. And people were saying, well, you can't very well beat him. He's tied to a lamp post, and he's tarred and feathered. We can't really add insult to injury. Or injury to the insult, maybe I should say.
<v Jackie Mcdonald>是的。其实那个女人想揍他。但人们说，别这样，你不能揍他。他被绑在灯柱上，还被浇了柏油、粘了羽毛。我们不能在伤口上撒盐了。或者说，不能在羞辱之上再施加伤害。

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:45.430
<v Ira Glass>Jon is talking here to Jackie McDonald, who is one of the leaders of the UDA, the Ulster Defense Association, which is a paramilitary group. During all the years of troubles in Northern Ireland, the paramilitary groups were the ones who would beat people up. They'd shoot people on both sides of the conflict. And even though most of the violence ended with a ceasefire in the 1990s, those groups are still around. They still exist. Jon says that Jackie McDonald looks and dresses a lot like Pauly from The Sopranos .
<v Ira Glass>乔恩在这里采访的是杰基·麦克唐纳，他是阿尔斯特防卫协会（UDA）的领导人之一，这是一个准军事组织。在北爱尔兰的动荡岁月里，准军事组织就是那些会殴打他人的人。他们会在冲突双方阵营里枪杀他人。尽管大多数暴力在90年代的停火协议后结束了，但这些组织依然存在。乔恩说，杰基·麦克唐纳的长相和打扮很像《黑道家族》里的保利。

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:56.400
<v Jon Ronson>Yeah, he had a big gold chain. He had thick, kind of Pauly hair. He had his shirt open, so you could see his chest hair. He had one of those leather jackets that tough guys wear.
<v Jon Ronson>对，他戴着一条粗金链子。头发是那种保利式的浓密发型。衬衫敞开着，能看到胸毛。他还穿着那种硬汉常穿的皮夹克。

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.570
<v Ira Glass>Did his organization do the tarring and feathering?
<v Ira Glass>他的组织干了这起柏油与羽毛的处罚吗？

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:07.370
<v Jon Ronson>Well, he kept on saying the community did it. But then when I pressed him, he said, well, we are the community leaders, the UDA, so yeah.
<v Jon Ronson>嗯，他一直说这是社区干的。但当我追问时，他说，好吧，我们是社区领导人，是UDA，所以，是的。

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:09.220
<v Ira Glass>Was he there?
<v Ira Glass>他在场吗？

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:09.650
<v Jon Ronson>Yeah.
<v Jon Ronson>在。

00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:12.530
<v Jon Ronson>By the way, were you here the day that they--
<v Jon Ronson>顺便问一下，他们那天……的时候你在场吗？

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:14.740
<v Jackie Mcdonald>I just happened to be walking past.
<v Jackie Mcdonald>我只是刚好路过。

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:19.460
<v Jon Ronson>Now when he said that, the woman who was standing with him--
<v Jon Ronson>他说这话时，站在他旁边的那个女人——

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:19.960
<v Ira Glass>A local.
<v Ira Glass>一个当地人。

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:27.660
<v Jon Ronson>A local lady-- burst into silent laughter, which I found quite telling.
<v Jon Ronson>当地的一位女士——突然无声地笑了起来，我觉得这很有深意。

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:38.480
<v Ira Glass>Now, people do all kinds of things in this world that might tick off a paramilitary group. So what does it take to get tarred and feathered? Like what exactly did this guy do?
<v Ira Glass>在这个世界上，人们会做各种可能惹恼准军事组织的事。那么，到底要干什么才会遭到柏油与羽毛的惩罚？这家伙到底做了什么？

00:02:38.480 --> 00:03:05.870
<v Jon Ronson>OK, well, according to the Daily Mail article that came out at the time, he'd been a bouncer in a city center bar. And he lost his job, and he was having marital difficulties. And so he started dealing drugs to children, according to the Daily Mail. And he was repeatedly told to stop, but he didn't stop. And then one day, word got around a local pub that he was dealing drugs to children in a park.
<v Jon Ronson>好吧，根据当时《每日邮报》的报道，他曾经是市中心酒吧的保安。他丢了工作，又遇到了婚姻问题。据《每日邮报》称，他开始向孩子们兜售毒品。他多次被警告停止，但他没停。直到有一天，当地一家酒吧传出消息，说他在公园里向孩子们卖毒品。

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:11.580
<v Jackie Mcdonald>And then he was caught, literally, with bags of coke in his pocket.
<v Jackie Mcdonald>然后，他被抓个正着，口袋里装着成袋的可卡因。

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:14.210
<v Ira Glass>Well, why not just go to the police, if they caught the guy with the cocaine?
<v Ira Glass>既然抓到了藏有可卡因的人，为什么不直接报警呢？

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:17.020
<v Jon Ronson>Right, well, Jackie told me why.
<v Jon Ronson>嗯，杰基告诉了我原因。

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:34.650
<v Jackie Mcdonald>The police need evidence, and the police need-- there's technicalities out there. And too many times in the past when drug dealers have been reported, they go to court, and they're back doing the same thing the same night. So the community just thought it was more serious than  that. It had to be dealt with in a different way.
<v Jackie Mcdonald>警察需要证据，而且警察需要……存在法律漏洞。过去很多次，毒贩被举报后送上法庭，结果当天晚上出来又重操旧业。所以社区认为这比那更严重。必须用不同的方式解决。

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:35.280
<v Jon Ronson>Right.
<v Jon Ronson>明白了。

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:43.350
<v Jackie Mcdonald>We actually talked about having somewhere in the park where we could put-- what do you call it?
<v Jackie Mcdonald>我们其实讨论过在公园里设个什么东西……你们叫它什么来着？

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:44.120
<v Jon Ronson>Like the stocks?
<v Jon Ronson>类似枷锁吗？

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:48.520
<v Jackie Mcdonald>Exactly. The stocks. We were going to have them there as a deterrent.
<v Jackie Mcdonald>没错，枷锁。我们本打算把它放在那儿作为威慑。

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:59.150
<v Ira Glass>Wait, wait, wait. I'm just going to stop the tape. He's saying stocks, like in colonial America, like where people would have their hands sticking through wood things, and they would just bring in stocks?
<v Ira Glass>等等，等等，让我停一下。他说枷锁，就像殖民地时期的美国那样，把人的手穿过木头，直接把枷锁带过来？

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:02.500
<v Jon Ronson>Yep. They were seriously considering bringing in stocks.
<v Jon Ronson>是的。他们真的考虑过带上枷锁。

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:18.560
<v Jackie Mcdonald>I'm going to talk this over with the police. When they say but if somebody was put in the stocks, and there was people throwing eggs or tomatoes at them, the police would have to intervene. And I says, well, if the police intervene, then the eggs and the tomatoes would be thrown at the police. So we didn't want to create that sort of situation.
<v Jackie Mcdonald>我会和警察谈谈这件事。当他们说，如果有人被关进枷锁，有人扔鸡蛋或西红柿，警察就必须介入。我说，如果警察介入，那鸡蛋和西红柿就会扔到警察身上。所以我们不想造成那种局面。

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:22.160
<v Jon Ronson>And in the end, they decided, OK, well, we'll tar and feather him.
<v Jon Ronson>最后，他们决定，好吧，就用柏油和羽毛惩罚他。

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:25.450
<v Ira Glass>So what exactly happens when you're tarred and feathered? Like, how does it go?
<v Ira Glass>那么，当遭遇柏油和羽毛惩罚时，具体会发生什么？流程是怎样的？

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:32.020
<v Jon Ronson>OK. So he was told to turn up at a certain hour. I think something like 11 o'clock in the morning on a Sunday morning.
<v Jon Ronson>好吧。他被告知在某个时间点出现。我想应该是周日上午11点左右。

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:35.630
<v Ira Glass>Wait, wait. They told him we're going to tar and feather you. Like you got to show up on time?
<v Ira Glass>等等，等等。他们告诉他，我们要用柏油和羽毛惩罚你。就像你必须按时出现一样？

00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:58.450
<v Jon Ronson>They didn't mention the words "tarring and feathering." They just said, you have to show up. You have to show up on Sunday morning at 11 o'clock. And so he did. Yeah, I know. He would have been pretty certain that something bad was going to happen to him. Nonetheless, he turned up on the dot of 11, because if you turn up late, something worse would happen to you.
<v Jon Ronson>他们没提“柏油与羽毛”这几个字。他们只是说，你必须到场。你必须在周日上午11点赶到。他就真的去了。是的，我知道。他一定很清楚会有可怕的事情发生在他身上。尽管如此，他还是在11点准时出现了，因为如果你迟到，会有更糟的事等着你。

00:04:58.450 --> 00:05:17.070
<v Ira Glass>Back, during the troubles, this would happen all the time. People would be asked to show up like this, and then they would get beaten with iron bars or baseball bats. Or they would get shot through the knees or ankles or hands. The paramilitary groups administered all kinds of punishments. So the guy shows up, as requested.
<v Ira Glass>在动荡时期，这种情况常有。人们会被要求这样现身，然后会被铁棍或棒球棍殴打。或者膝盖、脚踝或手被枪击。准军事组织执行各种惩罚。所以，那家伙按要求现身了。

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:18.520
<v Jon Ronson>And then what happened?
<v Jon Ronson>然后发生了什么？

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:25.840
<v Jackie Mcdonald>He was tied up, and a placard put on him. And he was tarred and feathered.
<v Jackie Mcdonald>他被绑住，身上挂了块牌子。然后被浇上柏油、粘上羽毛。

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:34.670
<v Jon Ronson>I asked them where they got the tar from. And they said, B and Q, which is the local DIY superstore. What's the American version of--
<v Jon Ronson>我问他们柏油是从哪儿弄来的。他们说，从B and Q，那是一家当地的家居建材超市。美国的对标店是……

00:05:34.670 --> 00:05:35.620
<v Ira Glass>That's like Home Depot.
<v Ira Glass>那是家得宝（Home Depot）。

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:38.280
<v Jon Ronson>Yeah, Home Depot. Yeah, our Home Depot.
<v Jon Ronson>对，家得宝。没错，就是我们的家得宝。

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:43.130
<v Jon Ronson>Were the feathers brought over in like a big bag? Or was it pillows?
<v Jon Ronson>羽毛是大袋装过来的吗？还是枕头里的？

00:05:43.130 --> 00:05:45.300
<v Jackie Mcdonald>I think it was a pillow.
<v Jackie Mcdonald>我想是一个枕头。

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:49.040
<v Jon Ronson>And would the pillow just have come from these houses just around here?
<v Jon Ronson>枕头是从这附近的人家拿的吗？

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:51.820
<v Jackie Mcdonald>Well, if they had asked for pillows, they would get 100.
<v Jackie Mcdonald>嗯，如果他们开口要枕头，能收到100个。

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:55.190
<v Ira Glass>I don't know if you can tell what he just said. He said, if they asked for pillows, they would've gotten 100.
<v Ira Glass>不知道你们能不能听出来刚才他说什么。他说，如果他们要枕头，就能收到100个。

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:59.990
<v Jackie Mcdonald>You know, the local people were very annoyed at what this fellow had done.
<v Jackie Mcdonald>要知道，当地人对这家伙干的事非常恼火。

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:22.490
<v Ira Glass>There are pictures of what this guy looked like, once he was tarred and feathered. And they are shocking. We put one on our website, if you're curious. He barely looks like a person, like he's just this blob of black tar and white feathers. You can't see his face at all. It's like his whole head is covered. He looks like the Abominable Snowman or something, with a placard hanging around his neck that says, I'm a drug-dealing scumbag.
<v Ira Glass>有这个家伙被浇上柏油和羽毛后的照片。看起来非常令人震惊。如果你好奇的话，我们把它贴在网站上了。他几乎看不出人样，就像一团黑色柏油和白色羽毛的混合体。你根本看不到他的脸。整个头都被覆盖了。他看起来像雪怪或者什么东西，脖子上挂着个牌子，写着：我是个兜售毒品的人渣。

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:43.750
<v Jon Ronson>So in the Daily Mail article that came out, that said that Jock Nelson screamed for mercy, as he was being tarred and feathered, that it was boiling tar, and that his shirt was pulled down over his shoulders so that it would burn. But Jackie says, it didn't happen quite that way and that Jock Nelson was fully clothed.
<v Jon Ronson>所以，《每日邮报》那篇报道说乔克·尼尔森在被惩罚时尖叫着求饶，说那是沸腾的柏油，他的衬衫被拉下肩膀，好让柏油烫伤他。但杰基说，情况并非如此，乔克·尼尔森当时穿着完整的衣服。

00:06:43.750 --> 00:06:48.470
<v Jon Ronson>Was he making any kind of noise, as the tar went over him?
<v Jon Ronson>当柏油浇在他身上时，他有没有发出什么声音？

00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:52.640
<v Jackie Mcdonald>No. No. Not that I could hear from a distance.
<v Jackie Mcdonald>没有。没有。至少我在远处没听到。

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:55.720
<v Jon Ronson>Right. OK. So he wasn't screaming or anything.
<v Jon Ronson>明白了。好。所以他没有尖叫什么的。

00:06:55.720 --> 00:07:00.030
<v Jackie Mcdonald>No, no. I think he was happy enough that that's all that was happening to him.
<v Jackie Mcdonald>没有，没有。我想他对自己只遭遇了这些还感到庆幸呢。

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:04.320
<v Jon Ronson>But he must have some kind of-- it must have been terrible.
<v Jon Ronson>但他一定经历了一些……那一定很可怕。

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:23.150
<v Jackie Mcdonald>Well, certainly, and it was meant to be. You have to remember, 10 years ago, this fellow would have been found, shot dead, [INAUDIBLE]. But because of the peace process and people moving on, things have been dealt with differently. And at the end of the day, tarring and feathering wasn't the worst thing could have happened. It could have been a lot worse.
<v Jackie Mcdonald>嗯，当然，而且目的就是要让他感到可怕。你要记住，10年前，这家伙会被人发现枪杀在某处。但由于和平进程和人们向前看，事情的处理方式不同了。最终，柏油与羽毛惩罚并不是可能发生的最坏结果。情况可能糟得多。

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:53.760
<v Ira Glass>Although the original story that we saw about this in the Daily Mail said that the guy who was tarred and feathered had been selling drugs to children, Jon came out of his visit to Belfast not sure that's true at all, or anyway, if it's the whole truth. A former BBC reporter named Brian Rowan, who's reported on Northern Ireland for over 20 years, pointed out to Jon that a guy like this one who was allegedly selling drugs might have been seen by the paramilitary groups as competition for their own drug operations.
<v Ira Glass>尽管我们看到的《每日邮报》关于这件事的原始报道说那个被惩罚的人向孩子兜售毒品，但乔恩在贝尔法斯特采访后，并不确定那是事实，或者至少不确定那是全部真相。一位前BBC记者布莱恩·罗恩（Brian Rowan）在北爱尔兰报道了20多年，他向乔恩指出，像这个涉嫌卖毒品的人，可能被准军事组织视为他们自己毒品生意的竞争对手。

00:07:53.760 --> 00:08:15.450
<v Brian Rowan>And while it is presented in this kind of Robin Hood fashion of defender of the community and helper of the community, these are paramilitary organizations who themselves are involved in drugs, are involved in crime. This is their way of presenting themselves in the light that they would like to present themselves in, that they're defenders of the community.
<v Brian Rowan>虽然这件事表现出一种罗宾汉式的、社区捍卫者或社区帮手的形象，但这些准军事组织本身就涉及毒品和犯罪。这就是他们展示自己的方式，他们想表现得像是社区的捍卫者。

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:31.340
<v Ira Glass>Keep in mind, also, that the allegation that Jock Nelson sold drugs is coming only from the paramilitary groups, that he never got due process. He got no day in court. We did reach out to him, to Jock Nelson, the guy who got tarred and feathered to get his side of the story.
<v Ira Glass>还要记住，乔克·尼尔森卖毒品的指控仅来自准军事组织，他从未得到正当程序。他没能上法庭辩护。我们确实联系了他，试图听听他的说法。

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:39.100
<v Jon Ronson>We tried. We contacted him on Twitter. And he just sent us a message saying he didn't want to talk to us. But good luck with our story.
<v Jon Ronson>我们试过了。我们在推特上联系了他。他只给我们回了一条消息，说他不想跟我们谈。但祝我们的报道好运。

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:40.640
<v Ira Glass>How's he doing?
<v Ira Glass>他现在怎么样了？

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:47.750
<v Jon Ronson>He's left Belfast for a while, went to Scotland. And now he's back working as a traffic warden.
<v Jon Ronson>他离开贝尔法斯特一段时间，去了苏格兰。现在他回来了，当了一名交通管理员。

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:50.500
<v Ira Glass>A traffic warden gives out parking tickets.
<v Ira Glass>交通管理员是开罚单的。

00:08:50.500 --> 00:09:02.830
<v Jon Ronson>And I said to Jackie McDonald, is he back selling drugs? And Jackie McDonald said, look, if he was back selling drugs, he wouldn't be a traffic warden right now. He'd be dead.
<v Jon Ronson>我问杰基·麦克唐纳，他有没有重操旧业卖毒品？杰基说，看，如果他还在卖毒品，他现在就当不了交通管理员了。他早就死了。

00:09:02.830 --> 00:09:12.140
<v Jon Ronson>You've been in prison yourself. So you know from both sides. What is the best deterrent? Is it going to prison or is it being tarred and feathered?
<v Jon Ronson>你自己也进过监狱，所以你了解双方的观点。什么才是最好的威慑？是进监狱，还是柏油与羽毛的惩罚？

00:09:12.140 --> 00:09:34.000
<v Jackie Mcdonald>Oh, tarred and feathering, without a doubt. I think, with ordinary decent criminals, [INAUDIBLE], drug dealers, thieves, hoods, whatever, there's something like 47%, 48%, 49% re-offend. With tarring and feathering, it's only had to happen once. There's been no re-offending since.
<v Jackie Mcdonald>哦，毫无疑问是柏油与羽毛惩罚。我认为，对于普通罪犯、毒贩、小偷、恶棍之流，再犯率大概有47%、48%、49%。而用了柏油与羽毛的惩罚，只需要一次就够了。此后再也没有重犯。

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:48.540
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our radio show, "Tarred and Feathered." Apparently, humiliating somebody in public is something lots of people enjoy. And it's also really effective. It gets results. People leave town. People change.
<v Ira Glass>好的，今天我们的电台节目主题是“柏油与羽毛”。显然，当众羞辱某人是很多人喜欢做的事情。而且它非常有效。它能带来结果。人们会离开城镇。人们会改变。

00:09:48.540 --> 00:10:11.460
<v Ira Glass>We have stories today of people who very much do not want public shaming. And they take action, dramatic action. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>我们今天有关于那些非常不希望被公众羞辱的人的故事。他们采取了行动，戏剧性的行动。来自WBEZ芝加哥，这是《美国生活》，由公共广播国际台发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:26.510
<v Ira Glass>Act One. The Hounds of Blairsville. There are all kinds of ways that you could be tarred and feathered, but the effects are pretty much the same, I think. People stop talking to you. You're a pariah. Stephanie Foo has this story from a small town in Georgia called Blairsville.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕：布莱尔斯维尔的猎犬。你可以通过各种方式遭遇柏油与羽毛的惩罚，但我认为效果大多是一样的。人们停止和你说话。你成了贱民。斯蒂芬妮·福（Stephanie Foo）带来了这个发生在佐治亚州布莱尔斯维尔小镇的故事。

00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:37.280
<v Stephanie Foo>Gene Cooley was planning his wedding. A second marriage for him and his fiancee Paulette. And one day he was at work cutting hair-- he's a hairstylist-- when he got a call from the police.
<v Stephanie Foo>吉恩·库利（Gene Cooley）正在筹划他的婚礼。这是他和未婚妻宝莱特（Paulette）的第二次婚姻。一天，他在工作时给人剪头发——他是个发型师——这时他接到了警察的电话。

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:51.660
<v Gene Cooley>And starts asking me about a shooting, of course, I knew nothing about. And all of a sudden, then they mention about Paulette. And I just totally freaked out at that time. So, give me a minute?
<v Gene Cooley>警察开始问我关于一起枪击案的事，我当然一无所知。突然，他们提到了宝莱特。那一刻我彻底崩溃了。能给我一点时间吗？

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:55.890
<v Stephanie Foo>Yeah, yeah, of course.
<v Stephanie Foo>好的，当然可以。

00:10:55.890 --> 00:11:23.770
<v Ira Glass>Paulette's ex-husband had murdered Paulette and then killed himself. He'd been unhappy about her remarrying. She'd filed a restraining order against him. He'd been violent before. Police called it an open-and-shut case. Gene, of course, was completely wrecked. He flew to her parents' house for the funeral. Her family welcomed him into their home, cooked for him, comforted him. Until Gene's second day there, when Paulette's dad started acting strangely.
<v Ira Glass>宝莱特的前夫谋杀了宝莱特，然后自杀了。他对她再婚很不满。她曾申请过限制令来防范他。他之前就有过暴力行为。警察把这定性为一起结案明确的谋杀自杀案。吉恩当然完全崩溃了。他飞到她父母家参加葬礼。她的家人欢迎他进家门，为他做饭，安慰他。直到吉恩在那里的第二天，宝莱特的父亲开始表现得很反常。

00:11:23.770 --> 00:11:40.140
<v Gene Cooley>I'd kind of slept in a little bit or I stayed in the room a little bit. I think it was probably about 9:00. He made a comment about oh, you always sleep this late? And I said, no, sir. He was a little on the cold side. And we started to have a little bit of breakfast.
<v Gene Cooley>我当时睡得稍微多了一点，或者在房间里待久了点。我想可能大约是9点吧。他评论说，哦，你总是睡得这么晚吗？我说，没有，先生。他显得有点冷淡。我们开始吃早饭。

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:57.120
<v Gene Cooley>And he started asking me about rehab. And I said whose rehab? And he said your rehab. And I told him I've never been in a rehab. What are you talking about? And he said, well, what about drug addictions? And I said, I don't have a drug addiction. I don't know what you're talking about.
<v Gene Cooley>然后他开始问我关于康复中心的事。我说谁的康复中心？他说，你的康复中心。我告诉他，我从来没去过康复中心。你在说什么？他说，那么，关于毒瘾呢？我说，我没有毒瘾。我不知道你在说什么。

00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:03.140
<v Stephanie Foo>Of course, that's exactly what you'd say if you were a drug addict. So Paulette's dad didn't find that very persuasive.
<v Stephanie Foo>当然，如果你是个瘾君子，你肯定会这么说。所以宝莱特的父亲觉得这话没多少说服力。

00:12:03.140 --> 00:12:06.440
<v Gene Cooley>And that's when they informed me that they wanted me out.
<v Gene Cooley>就在那时，他们通知我，他们想让我离开。

00:12:06.440 --> 00:12:28.390
<v Stephanie Foo>Gene had no idea where this was coming from, and didn't know how to prove that he'd never been an addict. Finally, out of desperation, he offered to go to the police, ask them to run a background check, print it out, and bring it back to prove he had never been in trouble with the law for drugs. Paulette's father said, great. Here are directions. Go do it now.
<v Stephanie Foo>吉恩完全不知道这事从哪儿冒出来的，也不知道怎么证明自己从来不是个瘾君子。最后，出于绝望，他提出去警察局，要求开一份背景调查，打印出来带回来，证明他从来没有因为毒品惹上过麻烦。宝莱特的父亲说，太好了。这是路线，现在就去办。

00:12:28.390 --> 00:12:50.270
<v Stephanie Foo>On the way, he called his sister in Blairsville to tell her Paulette's parents were acting really weird. And she said, something crazy is happening over here. Things are being said about you, bad things, all over the internet on something called Topix.
<v Stephanie Foo>在路上，他给他在布莱尔斯维尔的姐姐打了电话，告诉她宝莱特的父母表现得非常奇怪。她说，这边发生了一些疯狂的事情。关于你的事情被传开了，坏事，整个互联网上都在传，在一个叫Topix的网站上。

00:12:50.270 --> 00:13:15.000
<v Stephanie Foo>Topix is a website. If you live in a big city, chances are you've never even heard of it. But if you live in a small town, you probably know exactly what I'm talking about. Topix was originally supposed to be a local news site. But in practice, it's just a big message board where anybody can create an account and post a news item. And so more often than not, in places like Blairsville, it's mostly gossip.
<v Stephanie Foo>Topix是一个网站。如果你住在大城市，你可能根本没听说过它。但如果你住在小镇，你可能很清楚我在说什么。Topix最初被设想为一个本地新闻网站。但实际上，它就是一个巨大的留言板，任何人都可以注册账号并发布新闻。所以，在布莱尔斯维尔这样的地方，上面大多是流言蜚语。

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:33.520
<v Stephanie Foo>Blairsville's roughly a mile square. It's got a population of about 650. And that day, dozens of people were commenting on Gene's fiancee's murder. At first, the comments were sympathetic, people saying they felt sorry for Paulette and her children. And then the comments got ugly.
<v Stephanie Foo>布莱尔斯维尔大约有一平方英里大，人口约650人。那天，几十个人在评论吉恩未婚妻的谋杀案。起初，评论还很有同情心，人们说为宝莱特和她的孩子们感到难过。后来，评论变恶毒了。

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:51.930
<v Gene Cooley>There was like five or seven, I believe, user names being real hateful, real derogative, real just nasty on there. Calling me a pervert, making me out like I was a child molester, making me out like I was some kind of big drug user. You know, just total filth.
<v Gene Cooley>我想大概有五到七个用户名表现得非常憎恨，非常蔑视，非常恶心。叫我变态，把我描绘成猥亵儿童的人，把我描绘成某种大毒虫。你知道，全是脏水。

00:13:51.930 --> 00:14:07.420
<v Stephanie Foo>Someone with the user name Calvin asked, does anyone know the last name of Gene, the boyfriend hairstylist? I'm worried, because Gene is making his way down to Florida to meet with Paulette's side of the family. I'm truly fearful that this is not the end of this tragedy.
<v Stephanie Foo>一个用户名叫“卡尔文”（Calvin）的人问，有人知道那个发型师男友吉恩的姓吗？我很担心，因为吉恩正要去佛罗里达见宝莱特那边的家人。我真的害怕这悲剧还没结束。

00:14:07.420 --> 00:14:18.020
<v Stephanie Foo>Someone named Mouth then said, keep that creep away from the children. He is trouble. What would you do if the perv was chasing your grandchildren? Calvin thanked Mouth for the warning.
<v Stephanie Foo>一个叫“嘴巴”（Mouth）的人接着说，让那个变态离孩子远点。他是祸害。如果变态在追你的孙子，你会怎么办？卡尔文感谢“嘴巴”的警告。

00:14:18.020 --> 00:14:30.710
<v Stephanie Foo>And then someone who called himself Bugs added, Gene is not a nice guy. He cheated on his first wife. I know Paulette and Gene well, and they were both sickening out in public, kissing all over one another.
<v Stephanie Foo>然后一个自称“臭虫”（Bugs）的人补充道，吉恩不是个好人。他背叛了他的第一任妻子。我认识宝莱特和吉恩，他们在公开场合那种亲吻腻歪的样子真让人作呕。

00:14:30.710 --> 00:14:40.640
<v Stephanie Foo>It continued on like this. People accused him of every kind of character flaw you could imagine, of getting fired from every job he had, of being a liar, a drunk.
<v Stephanie Foo>评论就这样继续下去。人们指责他你能想象到的各种性格缺陷，说他丢了所有工作，是个骗子，是个酒鬼。

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:45.700
<v Gene Cooley>And I mean, to make accusations like that? I was wondering who the hell it was.
<v Gene Cooley>我的意思是，去做出那样的指控？我当时在想，这到底是谁干的。

00:14:45.700 --> 00:14:47.740
<v Stephanie Foo>Did you have any guesses?
<v Stephanie Foo>你有什么猜测吗？

00:14:47.740 --> 00:14:56.100
<v Gene Cooley>I had no idea who it could be. I've never been on a website like that. I don't really use a computer that much.
<v Gene Cooley>我完全不知道可能是谁。我从来没上过那种网站。我不怎么用电脑。

00:14:56.100 --> 00:15:22.360
<v Stephanie Foo>That's a huge understatement. Gene didn't know what YouTube was, until I explained it to him. It might not seem like a big deal that a couple of idiots are gossiping about you on the internet. But in a small town, it's different. You almost definitely know the people who wrote these things about you. And you know, the moment you walk outside your house, everyone you see has read it and is talking about it. Because in Blairsville, news travels fast.
<v Stephanie Foo>这话太含蓄了。吉恩在跟我解释YouTube之前，甚至不知道那是什么。几个白痴在网上八卦你，这看起来可能没什么大不了。但在一个小镇，情况就不同了。你几乎肯定认识那些写这些话的人。而且你知道，当你走出家门的那一刻，你看到的每一个人都读过这些内容，并且在谈论它。因为在布莱尔斯维尔，消息传播得极快。

00:15:22.360 --> 00:15:32.710
<v Gene Cooley>As a matter of fact, you being here for just a couple of hours before we met, I could almost tell you where you've been and what you even had for lunch.
<v Gene Cooley>事实上，你刚到这儿几个小时，我就几乎能说出你去过哪儿，甚至午饭吃的什么。

00:15:32.710 --> 00:15:35.260
<v Stephanie Foo>What do you mean? Like, just by asking other people?
<v Stephanie Foo>你什么意思？就像通过问其他人吗？

00:15:35.260 --> 00:15:44.130
<v Gene Cooley>You remember when you called me and said that you were about to have lunch? I'd already had two people call, and say, hey, did you know that there's a reporter over here in town?
<v Gene Cooley>你还记得当你给我打电话说你快要吃午饭的时候吗？我已经接到两个人的电话说，嘿，你知道镇上有个记者吗？

00:15:44.130 --> 00:15:47.150
<v Stephanie Foo>I didn't talk to anybody.
<v Stephanie Foo>我没和任何人说话啊。

00:15:47.150 --> 00:15:49.110
<v Stephanie Foo>Maybe somebody who was eating at the restaurant?
<v Stephanie Foo>也许是餐厅里吃饭的人？

00:15:49.110 --> 00:15:51.450
<v Gene Cooley>That's a big possibility.
<v Gene Cooley>那很有可能。

00:15:51.450 --> 00:16:15.240
<v Stephanie Foo>When I walked into the restaurant Gene was talking about, I had noticed that everyone turned and gave me the side eye. The big guys in hunting gear in the back, the tiny neat woman in the front. I sat there awkwardly, wondering whether it was because I was the only Chinese person in town. When the waitress came up and asked me for my order, the restaurant went silent to hear what it was.
<v Stephanie Foo>当我走进吉恩所说的那家餐厅时，我注意到每个人都转过头来，用斜眼打量我。后排那些穿着狩猎装备的大个子，前排那个瘦小的整洁女人。我尴尬地坐在那儿，纳闷是因为我是镇上唯一的华人吗。当服务员走过来问我要点什么时，整个餐厅都安静下来，想听听我会说什么。

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:29.570
<v Stephanie Foo>And this sort of talk, it's mostly harmless. But when Topix came onto the scene, it took gossip between friends, gossip that people would ordinarily recognize as just gossip, and made it into something more serious.
<v Stephanie Foo>这种谈话大多是无害的。但当Topix出现时，它把朋友间的八卦——人们通常会意识到这只是八卦——变成了一些更严重的东西。

00:16:29.570 --> 00:16:34.900
<v Stephanie Foo>This is Mark Cox. He's a friend of Gene's. He owns the hole-in-the-wall diner in town.
<v Stephanie Foo>这是马克·考克斯（Mark Cox）。他是吉恩的朋友。他在镇上经营一家小餐馆。

00:16:34.900 --> 00:16:45.860
<v Mark Cox>You could tell somebody something and they'll kind of believe you. But if they see it in writing, they're going to believe it. Once you write it down, it's not gossip anymore. You know, that becomes truth for what people are concerned with.
<v Mark Cox>你告诉某人一件事，他们会半信半疑。但如果他们看到写下来的文字，他们就会深信不疑。一旦写下来，它就不再是八卦了。你知道，对人们来说，那就成了真相。

00:16:45.860 --> 00:16:56.400
<v Stephanie Foo>Other people told me the same thing. Like this woman I met in town. She'd heard a friend gossiping about Gene, saying that he was a pervert, a child molester. She thought, yeah, right.
<v Stephanie Foo>其他人也告诉我同样的事。比如我在镇上遇到的那位女士。她曾听到一个朋友八卦吉恩，说他是变态，是猥亵儿童的人。她当时想，得了吧。

00:16:56.400 --> 00:17:20.250
<v Blairsville Neighbor>I remember. I mean, I can remember back things being said and I just brush it off and  go on. But when it's written there, and for anybody, I mean, and if it's on the web, then you kind of like, oh my gosh, is this true? Could this happen, you know? It puts that seed of doubt in your head. Of course.
<v Blairsville Neighbor>我记得。我的意思是，我记得以前说过的一些事，我只是置之不理。但当它被写在那里，对任何人来说，如果在网上，你就会想，天哪，这是真的吗？这可能发生吗？它会在你脑海中种下怀疑的种子。当然。

00:17:20.250 --> 00:17:38.820
<v Stephanie Foo>Gene did manage to calm his fiancee's parents down. But he was happy to go back home to Blairsville. He'd lived there for 16 years at that point, knew everybody. So when he got back, he was floored by the way people were suddenly treating him. Like, as if everything online was true.
<v Stephanie Foo>吉恩确实设法让未婚妻的父母平静了下来。但他很高兴能回到布莱尔斯维尔的家中。那时他在那儿住了16年，认识所有人。所以当他回来时，人们突然对待他的方式让他大吃一惊。就好像网上说的一切都是真的。

00:17:38.820 --> 00:18:00.520
<v Gene Cooley>OK, there was a gentleman that he and I used to pal around a little bit. Our children used to play together. I walked into a local convenience store and acknowledged him, told him hi. And he looked down at that point in time and just walked on out of the convenience store, got in his car and left.
<v Gene Cooley>好，有位先生，我和他以前经常混在一起。我们的孩子经常一起玩。我走进一家便利店向他打招呼，告诉他嗨。当时他低着头，直接走出了便利店，上了车就走了。

00:18:00.520 --> 00:18:04.310
<v Stephanie Foo>Or there was a guy he'd known for a while, who he ran into at a park.
<v Stephanie Foo>还有个他认识很久的人，在公园里偶遇。

00:18:04.310 --> 00:18:27.680
<v Gene Cooley>I just tried to have a little conversation with him. And he just told me pretty much get the F-word out of here. Get your perverted ass away from the kids. So I didn't know what to do at that point in time. I just left the-- I just had to go.
<v Gene Cooley>我只是试着跟他聊了两句。他却直接对我说，滚远点。带着你那变态的屁股滚离小孩远点。在那一刻我真不知道该怎么办。我只好离开——我只能走。

00:18:27.680 --> 00:18:51.910
<v Gene Cooley>I mean, the feeling was atrocious. I felt like I was a ghost. When your friends aren't talking to you, when people aren't even looking at you, I mean, it's at your bottom's end, right there. I mean, you feel worse than the manure laying on the damn ground. Because people at least step in manure, but they want to walk around you like crazy.
<v Gene Cooley>我的意思是，那种感觉简直糟糕透顶。我觉得自己像个鬼魂。当你的朋友不跟你说话，当人们甚至看都不看你一眼，我的意思是，这简直是人生谷底。我觉得自己比地上的粪便还不如。因为人们至少会踩在粪便上，但他们却像躲避瘟神一样躲着我。

00:18:51.910 --> 00:19:00.090
<v Stephanie Foo>Gene tried to get back to work at his hair salon, but all his clients canceled their appointments. Here's Gene's boss, Tony Smelt.
<v Stephanie Foo>吉恩试图回到发廊工作，但所有客人都取消了预约。这是吉恩的老板，托尼·斯梅尔特（Tony Smelt）。

00:19:00.090 --> 00:19:23.890
<v Tony Smelt>There were rumors going around that Gene may have done some drugs in his past, and that we were supplying him, and that my wife and I were drug-dealing pagans. I don't know where the information came from. I mean, we just certainly don't do drugs, and we're not pagans. We eat organic food, so--
<v Tony Smelt>到处有流言说吉恩过去可能吸过毒，我们在供给他，我和我妻子是卖毒品的异教徒。我不知道这些信息是从哪儿来的。我的意思是，我们肯定不碰毒品，我们也不是异教徒。我们吃有机食品，所以……

00:19:23.890 --> 00:19:46.710
<v Stephanie Foo>Tony is obviously not a local. He's from England. And he was new to town at that point. His business had only been around a year, and he couldn't afford trouble. Tony said people had even called the salon, asking if Gene was there, and saying they wouldn't come in anymore if he was. One woman said she was afraid that Gene might shoot her. So Tony asked Gene not to come into work anymore.
<v Stephanie Foo>托尼显然不是本地人。他来自英国。当时他刚到镇上。他的店才开了一年，他担不起麻烦。托尼说，甚至有人打电话到沙龙，问吉恩在不在，并说如果他在，他们就不来了。有一个女人说她担心吉恩可能会枪杀她。所以托尼请求吉恩以后不要再来上班了。

00:19:46.710 --> 00:19:53.140
<v Tony Smelt>Topix is like a virus. People are scared of that website. I am, that's for sure.
<v Tony Smelt>Topix就像病毒。人们害怕那个网站。我肯定是的。

00:19:53.140 --> 00:20:09.310
<v Stephanie Foo>Gene couldn't find another job in town. 2 and 1/2 months after his fiancee's death, he decided he'd had enough. Gene packed up to move to Augusta, 3 and 1/2 hours away. But he made one last stop on the way out of town, the office of Russell Stookey.
<v Stephanie Foo>吉恩在镇上找不到另一份工作。未婚妻死后两个半月，他决定受够了。吉恩打包准备搬去3个半小时车程外的奥古斯塔。但在离开城镇前，他最后去了一个地方，罗素·斯图基（Russell Stookey）的办公室。

00:20:09.310 --> 00:20:11.560
<v Stephanie Foo>Hi, Mr. Stookey.
<v Stephanie Foo>嗨，斯图基先生。

00:20:11.560 --> 00:20:13.150
<v Russell Stookey>Hi, are you Jennifer?
<v Russell Stookey>嗨，你是詹妮弗吗？

00:20:13.150 --> 00:20:13.570
<v Stephanie Foo>Stephanie.
<v Stephanie Foo>是斯蒂芬妮。

00:20:13.570 --> 00:20:14.400
<v Russell Stookey>Stephanie, I'm sorry.
<v Russell Stookey>斯蒂芬妮，抱歉。

00:20:14.400 --> 00:20:14.820
<v Stephanie Foo>Close enough.
<v Stephanie Foo>差不多。

00:20:14.820 --> 00:20:17.790
<v Russell Stookey>Come on. You're the first China lady I've ever met.
<v Russell Stookey>来吧。你是我见过的第一个中国女士。

00:20:17.790 --> 00:20:18.470
<v Stephanie Foo>China lady?
<v Stephanie Foo>中国女士？

00:20:18.470 --> 00:20:19.980
<v Russell Stookey>China lady.
<v Russell Stookey>中国女士。

00:20:19.980 --> 00:20:21.750
<v Stephanie Foo>OK.
<v Stephanie Foo>好的。

00:20:21.750 --> 00:20:37.770
<v Ira Glass>Russell's a sweet guy. But he does say exactly what's on his mind, which actually made him the perfect man to take Gene's case, because when Russell heard Gene's story, he despised the whole idea of anonymous posters. And all he wanted to do is give them hell.
<v Ira Glass>罗素是个可爱的人。但他说话直来直去，这反而使他成为受理吉恩案的最佳人选，因为当罗素听到吉恩的故事时，他鄙视匿名发帖的那套做法。他只想让他们付出代价。

00:20:37.770 --> 00:21:06.670
<v Russell Stookey>They have no character. That means they have no guts. They have no guts, no character. They lack courage, no balls at all. (WHISPERED)  Should I say balls? Anyway, I'm old school. If I've got something to say, I'll say it to your damn face. [GUFFAWS] That's just the way it is. You see these scars on my face? A lot of times, people don't like that. And they'll punch you. But that's their opportunity. And that's the way you do business in this life. You say it to their face.
<v Russell Stookey>他们没有品格。这意味着他们没胆量。他们没胆量，没品格。他们缺乏勇气，一点骨气都没有。（低声）我该说“蛋蛋”吗？反正，我是老派人。如果我有话要说，我会当面告诉你。（大笑）事情就是这样。你看到我脸上的伤疤了吗？很多时候，人们不喜欢那样。他们会揍你。但这正是他们的机会。这就是这辈子做事的规矩。你要当面说出来。

00:21:06.670 --> 00:21:31.630
<v Stephanie Foo>The case was tricky. Topix isn't liable for what its users post. And they wouldn't tell Russell the true identities of the posters. It took a year and a half, but eventually a case in Texas went through. A federal judge ruled that Topix had to give up the true identities of any slanderous posters. So Russell used that ruling to find out who the anonymous posters really were. And what he found shocked him.
<v Stephanie Foo>案子很棘手。Topix不对其用户的发布内容承担责任。他们不会告诉罗素这些发帖者的真实身份。这花了一年半的时间，最终德克萨斯州的一个案件取得了进展。一位联邦法官裁定，Topix必须交出任何诽谤性发帖者的真实身份。所以罗素利用那个裁决查出了这些匿名发帖者到底是谁。他发现的内容让他大吃一惊。

00:21:31.630 --> 00:21:57.690
<v Stephanie Foo>The nastiest posters were actually just one person. Calvin, Mouth, Bugs, who were all going back and forth on Topix, talking about Gene and thanking each other for their warnings about him-- they were all the same woman, a woman who had gone to the trouble of making multiple accounts and then having fake conversations between those accounts. Her name was Sybil Denise Ballew. And when Gene heard her name--
<v Stephanie Foo>最恶毒的发帖者其实只有一个人。卡尔文、“嘴巴”、“臭虫”，这些在Topix上互相往来，谈论吉恩并互相感谢对方警告的人——她们全都是同一个女人，一个费尽心思创建多个账号，然后让这些账号之间虚假对话的女人。她的名字叫西比尔·丹妮丝·巴柳（Sybil Denise Ballew）。当吉恩听到她的名字时——

00:21:57.690 --> 00:22:00.100
<v Gene Cooley>I had no idea who it was.
<v Gene Cooley>我完全不知道那是谁。

00:22:00.100 --> 00:22:24.680
<v Stephanie Foo>Gene was totally baffled. It wasn't until much later that he learned that over 10 years ago, he and Sybil Ballew had both worked at a department store in town called Alexander's. Most people in town call it the Hillbilly Walmart, though it's more like a general store. Gene says he only worked there for three months, mostly in the warehouse. He barely remembers it. But Sybil says she remembers him perfectly.
<v Stephanie Foo>吉恩彻底困惑了。直到很久以后，他才知道，10多年前，他和西比尔·巴柳都在镇上一家叫亚历山大（Alexander's）的百货商店工作过。镇上大多数人把它叫“山里人的沃尔玛”，尽管它更像个杂货店。吉恩说他只在那儿工作了三个月，主要在仓库。他几乎不记得了。但西比尔说她对他记忆犹新。

00:22:24.680 --> 00:22:44.880
<v Sybil Ballew>I don't like the way he looked at the younger girls in staff where we worked together. Looking them up and down, lusty look. You know what I'm saying? There's a difference in looking, and there is a difference in (ELONGATING) "looooking."
<v Sybil Ballew>我不喜欢他看我们一起工作的年轻女孩的方式。从上到下打量，那种淫荡的目光。你懂我的意思吗？“看”和“打……量”是有区别的。

00:22:44.880 --> 00:22:51.680
<v Stephanie Foo>Sybil talked to me on the phone. She refused to see me when I was in Blairsville. And to this day, she believes Gene had it coming.
<v Stephanie Foo>西比尔在电话里跟我聊过。当我在布莱尔斯维尔时，她拒绝见我。直到今天，她依然认为吉恩是罪有应得。

00:22:51.680 --> 00:23:37.290
<v Sybil Ballew>He's the reason the woman's dead. He is the very reason that woman is dead. He knew how her (EMPHASIS) "husband" was. But yet, he kept doing what he was doing. He'd come in there with her on numerous times. Sit in the corner, and that woman couldn't even eat for him pawing at her, being gross. You know what I'm saying? You don't do stuff like that out in public, for God's sake. People went back and told the ex-husband to get the ex-husband riled up and disturbed enough about it to kill the woman.
<v Sybil Ballew>那个女人死就是因为他。那个女人死完全是因为他。他知道她“丈夫”是什么样的人。但他还是继续做他做的事。他多次带她进那儿。坐在角落里，那女人因为他毛手毛脚、举止粗俗甚至连饭都吃不下。你知道我在说什么吗？看在上帝的份上，你不能在公共场合做那种事。人们回去告诉了前夫，把前夫激怒了，闹得动静大到让他杀了那女人。

00:23:37.290 --> 00:23:42.110
<v Stephanie Foo>What business is it of yours, though? I mean, it seems like you're making a lot of assumptions.
<v Stephanie Foo>但这关你什么事呢？我的意思是，你好像做了很多假设。

00:23:42.110 --> 00:23:51.300
<v Sybil Ballew>Did you not understand or listen to what I said? He brought it upon himself in my opinion.
<v Sybil Ballew>你没听懂或没听我说的话吗？在我看来，是他自找的。

00:23:51.300 --> 00:23:53.270
<v Stephanie Foo>Are you proud of what you did?
<v Stephanie Foo>你为你所做的事感到自豪吗？

00:23:53.270 --> 00:24:10.220
<v Sybil Ballew>[SCOFFS] Am I proud of what I did? I'm proud of standing up for what I believe in, for what I know. I'm proud of telling the truth.
<v Sybil Ballew>（嗤笑）我为你所做的事感到自豪吗？我为坚持我所相信的、所知道的事情感到自豪。我为说出真相感到自豪。

00:24:10.220 --> 00:24:17.610
<v Stephanie Foo>Gene sued Sybil Ballew for libel and defamation of character. Again, here's Gene's lawyer, Russell Stookey.
<v Stephanie Foo>吉恩以诽谤和损害名誉的罪名起诉了西比尔·巴柳。再次提一下，这是吉恩的律师，罗素·斯图基。

00:24:17.610 --> 00:24:46.590
<v Russell Stookey>So I had Mrs. Ballew on the stand. And to show what kind of legal scholar she is, I said-- she kept telling us about the First Amendment protected her. And I said, Ms. Ballew, you mentioned the First Amendment. Do you know what the First Amendment says? What is the first word in the First Amendment of the Constitution of the United States of America? She didn't know that. Here's a flash for you, morons. The First Amendment does not cover all speech. Defamation is not ever protected speech.
<v Russell Stookey>所以我在庭上质问了巴柳太太。为了显示她是个什么样的“法律学者”，我说——她一直在跟我们念叨第一修正案保护她。我说，巴柳女士，你提到了第一修正案。你知道第一修正案说了什么吗？美国宪法第一修正案的第一个词是什么？她不知道。给你们提个醒，白痴们。第一修正案并不涵盖所有言论。诽谤从来不是受保护的言论。

00:24:46.590 --> 00:25:09.070
<v Stephanie Foo>Russell's right. Libel is against the law. And if the lies hurt the victim financially, that person is entitled to receive payback. The jury ruled that Sybil Denise Ballew owed Gene $404,000, with $250,000 in punitive damages. It was the first successful lawsuit lodged against an anonymous Topix poster.
<v Stephanie Foo>罗素是对的。诽谤是非法的。如果谎言在经济上伤害了受害者，当事人有权获得补偿。陪审团裁定，西比尔·丹妮丝·巴柳需赔偿吉恩40.4万美元，其中25万美元为惩罚性赔偿。这是针对匿名Topix发帖者的首个胜诉案例。

00:25:09.070 --> 00:25:16.430
<v Russell Stookey>[EXHALES] God almighty. He went home that night, feeling like a man.
<v Russell Stookey>（呼气）天哪。他那天晚上回家时，感觉自己像个真正的男人。

00:25:16.430 --> 00:25:33.430
<v Stephanie Foo>It wasn't a complete triumph. The case was expensive, and Gene will probably never see any sort of reimbursement for that. To this day, Sybil has not paid any of the $404,000 to Gene. Russell says she just does not have that kind of money.
<v Stephanie Foo>这不是一个彻底的胜利。诉讼费用昂贵，而且吉恩可能永远看不到任何赔偿。直到今天，西比尔还没付给吉恩那40.4万中的任何一分钱。罗素说她根本拿不出那么多钱。

00:25:33.430 --> 00:25:52.460
<v Stephanie Foo>But Gene got what was most important to him. He moved back to Blairsville after the case, back to his family, his friends, his kids. And, of course, everyone had heard about the verdict by the time he got back. And people started talking to him again like nothing had happened, like that guy in the convenience store, the guy who walked out when Gene tried to talk to him?
<v Stephanie Foo>但吉恩得到了对他来说最重要的一切。案子结束后他搬回了布莱尔斯维尔，回到了家人、朋友、孩子身边。当然，他回来时，大家都听说了判决结果。人们又开始像什么都没发生一样和他说话了，比如那个在便利店的人，那个吉恩试图跟他说话时走开的家伙？

00:25:52.460 --> 00:26:04.040
<v Gene Cooley>I saw him the other day at a convenience store. And he greeted me. Hi Gene, how are you doing? It's good seeing you. We shook hands, and I'll talk to you later. I'll see you around.
<v Gene Cooley>前几天我在便利店看到他。他跟我打招呼。嗨，吉恩，你好吗？见到你真好。我们握了手，我会再和你聊。回头见。

00:26:04.040 --> 00:26:08.760
<v Stephanie Foo>You must have some sort of feeling like, oh, now you want to talk to me?
<v Stephanie Foo>你一定有种感觉，哦，现在你想跟我说话了？

00:26:08.760 --> 00:26:32.770
<v Gene Cooley>Yes. [RUEFUL LAUGH] Yeah, that is kind of how I did feel, to be honest with you. I see everybody a lot differently now that I've been through all this. I just look at different people more cautious, instead of letting them into my life, letting them into my heart. I never thought that people could be so cruel.
<v Gene Cooley>是的。（自嘲地笑）说实话，我的感觉确实是那样。经历了这一切后，我看每个人都大不相同了。我只是更谨慎地看待不同的人，不再随意让他们进入我的生活、进入我的内心。我从没想过人们会如此残忍。

00:26:32.770 --> 00:26:54.480
<v Stephanie Foo>I asked around town to see if stories like Gene's were common. And almost everyone knew someone who'd been hurt by posts on Topix. One woman told me that someone wrote a post saying her coworker was having an affair. She said her co-worker's marriage fell apart because of it. And her kids were teased at school. Another said her husband was slandered, which hurt the family business.
<v Stephanie Foo>我在镇上打听了一下，看看吉恩这样的故事是否常见。几乎每个人都认识受Topix帖子伤害的人。一位女士告诉我，有人写帖子说她的同事在搞外遇。她说她同事的婚姻因此破裂了。她的孩子在学校也受到嘲弄。另一位说她的丈夫被诽谤了，这伤害了家族生意。

00:26:54.480 --> 00:27:05.040
<v Stephanie Foo>But after Gene's case, after people found out they could sue for slander, lots of people started to call up lawyers. Russell Stookey says he probably gets a call every month.
<v Stephanie Foo>但在吉恩案之后，在人们发现可以起诉诽谤后，很多人开始打电话找律师。罗素·斯图基说他每个月大概能接到一个电话。

00:27:05.040 --> 00:27:35.890
<v Russell Stookey>Normally, you're talking about bored housewives who are just gossip-mongers, basically. But it is an epidemic. I have caught lawyers. I caught a judge. I've caught doctors, a dentist, a state representative. I found out in a heartbeat who these people were. I sued them. And you ought to hear them scream and cry. Let me go. Let me out of this. You did it.
<v Russell Stookey>通常，这都是些无聊的家庭主妇，说白了就是八卦传播者。但这已经是一种流行病了。我抓住过律师。我抓住过法官。我抓住过医生、牙医、州议员。我瞬间就查到了这些人是谁。我起诉了他们。你应该听听他们尖叫、哭喊的样子。放过我吧。让我从这事里解脱吧。你们做得太绝了。

00:27:35.890 --> 00:27:56.080
<v Stephanie Foo>If you go on to Blairsville's Topix page today, there are only a dozen or so regular posters left. It's considered an embarrassment now. And nobody really reads the gossip anymore. Or anyway, if they do, nobody wants to admit it.
<v Stephanie Foo>如果你今天登录布莱尔斯维尔的Topix页面，只剩下十几条常规帖子了。这现在被视为一种尴尬。也没人真正再去看那些八卦了。或者说，即使看，也没人愿意承认。

00:27:56.080 --> 00:28:29.720
<v Ira Glass>Stephanie Foo is one of the producers of our program. Coming up, we move from a story where the internet is causing huge problems in somebody's life to a story where the internet is actually helping somebody solve a huge problem. That's in a minute. From Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>斯蒂芬妮·福是我们节目的制作人之一。接下来，我们将从一个互联网给某人生活带来巨大麻烦的故事，过渡到一个互联网确实帮助某人解决巨大麻烦的故事。一分钟后回来。来自芝加哥公共广播和公共广播国际台，节目继续。

00:28:29.720 --> 00:28:48.020
<v Ira Glass>We've arrived at Act Two of our program. Act Two, Help Wanted. So this story is about a group that's pretty universally reviled. I think if there ever was a group that people would want to tar and feather, this would be the group. And that's pedophiles. Though I have to say, before we start, this is not the story you usually hear about pedophiles.
<v Ira Glass>我们进入了节目的第二幕。第二幕，招聘启事。这个故事是关于一个普遍被唾弃的群体。我想如果这世上真有什么群体是人们想用“柏油与羽毛”惩罚的，那一定就是这个群体。那就是恋童癖者。不过我必须在开始之前说，这不是你通常听到的那种关于恋童癖者的故事。

00:28:48.020 --> 00:29:03.880
<v Ira Glass>Reporter Luke Malone met somebody who has those feelings for young children and definitely does not want to act on them, and has to cobble together his own way to deal with the problem which raises the question is there a way to treat pedophiles. Could there be a way to treat pedophiles?
<v Ira Glass>记者卢克·马龙（Luke Malone）见到一个人，他对幼童有这种感觉，但绝对不想付诸行动，而且必须想尽办法自己去处理这个问题，这提出了一个问题：有没有办法治疗恋童癖者？是否存在治疗恋童癖者的方法？

00:29:03.880 --> 00:29:20.390
<v Ira Glass>A warning, before we start, this story is definitely not for kids. Most of the names have been changed and two of the people in this story have asked to have their voices altered. Also, though there's nothing graphic in this story at all, victims of child sexual abuse should consider this a trigger warning.
<v Ira Glass>开始前有个警告，这个故事绝对不适合儿童收听。大多数名字都经过了修改，故事中的两个人要求将他们的声音进行处理。此外，虽然这个故事中没有任何露骨的内容，但儿童性虐待的受害者应将此视为一个触发预警。

00:29:20.390 --> 00:29:34.540
<v Ira Glass>Everybody else, I will say, I have never heard anybody talk about these issues the way that the people in this story talk about them. I feel like I came out of hearing this story understanding something I really did not know before. So here's Luke Malone.
<v Ira Glass>至于其他人，我会说，我从未听过有人像这个故事中的人们那样讨论这些问题。我觉得听完这个故事，我理解了一些以前确实不知道的东西。下面请听卢克·马龙的报道。

00:29:34.540 --> 00:30:00.780
<v Luke Malone>My interest in pedophiles began in the wake of the Jerry Sandusky trial. He had just been sentenced on 45 counts related to child sex abuse. It got me thinking about how all this starts and if there's a way to prevent it. I mean, Sandusky wasn't born a 68-year-old child molester. How old are pedophiles when they start thinking about kids this way? Do they ever want to do something to stop themselves? And if they do, who can they turn to?
<v Luke Malone>我对恋童癖者的兴趣始于杰瑞·桑达斯基（Jerry Sandusky）庭审之后。他刚刚因为与儿童性虐待相关的45项指控被判刑。这让我思考这一切是如何开始的，以及是否有办法预防。我的意思是，桑达斯基不是一出生就是68岁的猥亵儿童者。恋童癖者在什么年龄开始以这种方式看待孩子？他们是否有过阻止自己的念头？如果有，他们能向谁求助？

00:30:00.780 --> 00:30:21.300
<v Luke Malone>I spoke to experts and dug around online. I came across a site, a group of people who acknowledged their attraction, but want help dealing with it. Most of the men I spoke with first noticed an interest in children when they were about 13 or 14. I had no idea. I asked how they reacted when they first knew something was up.
<v Luke Malone>我采访了专家并上网搜索。我偶然发现了一个网站，一群承认自己有这种吸引力，但想要寻求帮助来处理它的人。我采访的大多数男性在13或14岁左右时第一次注意到对儿童的兴趣。我完全没想到。我问他们第一次察觉到不对劲时有什么反应。

00:30:21.300 --> 00:30:36.800
<v Luke Malone>For most of them, this was going back 40 or 50 years. And I realized that I needed to speak with younger guys, ones who were going through this right now. I asked the men if they knew anyone like that. And a week or so later, I got an email.
<v Luke Malone>对他们大多数人来说，那是40或50年前的事了。我意识到我需要采访一些年轻人，那些现在正经历这些的人。我问那些男士是否认识这样的人。一周左右后，我收到了一封电子邮件。

00:30:36.800 --> 00:31:00.850
<v Luke Malone>My name is Adam, it read. I'm 18, and I'm the leader of a support group for pedophiles around my age. I'll be very happy to talk with you. Adam is now 19. Just to remind you, Adam isn't his real name. And his voice has been altered to protect his identity. But even knowing he'd be anonymous, he was uncomfortable.
<v Luke Malone>“我叫亚当，”信中写道，“我18岁，是一个同龄恋童癖互助小组的负责人。我很乐意和你谈谈。”亚当现在19岁。提醒一下，亚当不是他的真名。他的声音经过处理以保护身份。但即使知道会匿名，他还是很不安。

00:31:00.850 --> 00:31:02.550
<v Adam>You know, nervous.
<v Adam>你知道，很紧张。

00:31:02.550 --> 00:31:03.880
<v Luke Malone>Why?
<v Luke Malone>为什么？

00:31:03.880 --> 00:31:20.120
<v Adam>Just don't think I'm a very vulgar person. It's even weird for me to say it out loud. You know, it's something I type, probably 50 times a day, just chatting with some people online, but actually saying it out loud is not very easy for me.
<v Adam>就是不觉得我是个很粗俗的人。连大声说出来对我来说都很奇怪。你知道，这只是我打字会用的东西，可能每天聊天时会打50次，但实际上大声说出来对我来说并不容易。

00:31:20.120 --> 00:31:23.130
<v Luke Malone>Do you see yourself as a pedophile?
<v Luke Malone>你把自己看作恋童癖者吗？

00:31:23.130 --> 00:31:25.260
<v Adam>Uh, yes.
<v Adam>嗯，是的。

00:31:25.260 --> 00:31:27.200
<v Luke Malone>And have you ever acted on your attraction?
<v Luke Malone>你有没有根据你的吸引力采取过行动？

00:31:27.200 --> 00:31:29.120
<v Adam>No.
<v Adam>没有。

00:31:29.120 --> 00:31:46.560
<v Luke Malone>Here's the first of many distinctions, I wasn't clear on when I first met Adam. And it's an important one to make. Technically, you don't have to act on your desires to be a pedophile. Pedophilia marks the attraction, not the behavior. Adam doesn't want to act on his attractions.
<v Luke Malone>这是我第一次见到亚当时不清楚的众多区别之一。这是一个很重要的区别。从技术上讲，你不必根据你的欲望去行动就能成为恋童癖者。恋童癖标志的是一种吸引力，而不是行为。亚当不想采取行动。

